 — MapReduce (confidence: 0.75)\nWhy this fits: The task involves mapping over a set of seasonal menu items to perform calculations and then producing a final output. MapReduce is suitable here because the nutritional calculations (calories, carbs, etc.) and allergen identification are per-item tasks. The 'mechanical reduction' mention in avoid-when for other classes suggests MapReduce is better when the aggregation is structured, though the intent asks for per-serving labels rather than a single reduction.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nMapReduce is a 3-stage shape: :chunk-document → :map-each [:llm] → :code reducer. The reducer is deterministic Clojure (count, sum, dedup, flatten, group-by) rather than LLM synthesis, saving tokens and eliminating aggregation ambiguity. Pair the per-chunk :llm with :output-schemas so the reducer's destructuring is safe. Choose MapReduce over ChunkedExtraction when the cross-chunk combine is mechanical; choose ChunkedExtraction when it requires natural-language judgment.\n\n\n#### Alternative #3 — MapReduce (confidence: 0.75)\nWhy this fits: The task involves mapping over a set of seasonal menu items to perform calculations and then producing a final output. MapReduce is suitable here because the nutritional calculations (calories, carbs, etc.) and allergen identification are per-item tasks. The 'mechanical reduction' mention in avoid-when for other classes suggests MapReduce is better when the aggregation is structured, though the intent asks for per-serving labels rather than a single reduction.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nMapReduce is a 3-stage shape: :chunk-document → :map-each [:llm] → :code reducer. The reducer is deterministic Clojure (count, sum, dedup, flatten, group-by) rather than LLM synthesis, saving tokens and eliminating aggregation ambiguity. Pair the per-chunk :llm with :output-schemas so the reducer's destructuring is safe. Choose MapReduce over ChunkedExtraction when the cross-chunk combine is mechanical; choose ChunkedExtraction when it requires natural-language judgment.\n\n\n#### Alternative #4 — Parallel-classify-aggregate (confidence: 0.70)\nWhy this fits: The task requires allergen identification (classification) and metric calculation. This hybrid pattern allows the LLM to handle the natural language 'allergen' judgment per chunk/item while a code reducer could handle the deterministic scaling of nutrients. However, the task isn't just about a 'category distribution', which is this class's primary strength.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nParallel-classify-aggregate is the hybrid MapReduce variant: chunk → :llm classifier per-chunk → :code reducer for counts. The :llm scope stays narrow (single category label per chunk) while the :code reducer handles deterministic counting. Always declare :output-schemas with an :enum on the classifier to prevent label drift. Always bound :map-each :max-concurrency to 3 (LLM rate limits). The code reducer must explicitly handle nil/missing labels. Use parallel-sum when the per-chunk work is arithmetic; use chunked-extraction when cross-chunk reasoning is needed.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :chunk-document → :map-each with :llm classifier per-chunk → :code grouping reducer\n  - natural for any 'classify each chunk into a category and count by category' work — sentiment distribution, error-type histogram, topic mix\n  - outputs are typically a per-category count map plus optionally per-category exemplars\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** splitting LLM classification (map) from code aggregation (reduce) keeps the LLM scope narrow per chunk — each per-chunk call only needs to emit a single category label, not reason about the full document (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: each chunk's classification is independent AND the final analysis is a category distribution\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :document :size 1500 :into :chunks}] [:map-each {:from :chunks :as :chunk :into :classifications :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:category] :output-schemas {:category [:enum :positive :negative :neutral]}}]] [:code {:reads [:classifications] :writes [:distribution] :fn (fn [{:keys [classifications]}] {:distribution (frequencies (map :category classifications))})}] [:final {:keys [:distribution]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** always declare :output-schemas with an :enum on the per-chunk :llm classifier — free-form string labels drift across chunks (\"positive\" vs \"Positive\" vs \"pos\") and break the code reducer's frequencies (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the category set is small and pre-specified\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:writes [:category] :output-schemas {:category [:enum :positive :negative :neutral]}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** unbounded :max-concurrency on the per-chunk :llm classifier hits provider rate limits on documents with 8+ chunks — same as document-analysis (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: :max-concurrency is unset on :map-each AND the chunk count exceeds 8\n    - Recommended fix: always bound :max-concurrency to 3 on :map-each parents of per-chunk :llm calls — keeps within provider rate budgets even on long documents\n\n  - **Failure mode:** code reducer that assumes :category will be present silently produces wrong counts when the LLM returns nil/missing labels (schema validation skipped or per-chunk failure) (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the code reducer assumes per-chunk schema-validated output without checking for missing labels\n    - Recommended fix: the reducer should explicitly handle missing classifications (e.g., add :unknown to the enum AND map nil → :unknown before frequencies) OR fail loudly with a per-chunk diagnostic\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - sentiment distribution across a long product-review document: per-paragraph sentiment → category counts\n  - error-type histogram across a chunked log file: per-chunk error classification → type counts\n  - topic mix in a research paper: per-section topic classification → topic distribution\n\nAssigned to domain child 8d4e82c6-f9ae-3795-9d34-631e4d128a11 (label: nutritional-label-generation) under the top match — this campaign's outcome is that child's first evidence.\nCapabilities:\n  - Domain family born for a task whose subject matter, material and output kind are: The candidate's strengths describe a pattern where input requires multiple distinct transformation passes producing named intermediates (normalized, enriched, formatted), which fits the flow from ingredient list -> nutritional metrics -> formatted labels. While not a direct match for 'nutrition', its 'good-when' for mechanical formatting of enriched data matches the task's output requirement.\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** emits the 1558197243 worked tree for tasks of this class (confidence 0.40, evidence-count 0)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:instruction \"Extract and calculate raw nutritional metrics (Calories, Protein, Carbs, Fats) for the bulk quantities provided in the ingredient list using the USDA reference sheet. Process: Grain Bowl, Side Salad, and Protein Snack Pack. Also identify all major allergens (Milk, Eggs, Fish, Shellfish, Tree Nuts, Peanuts, Wheat, Soybeans, Sesame) present in each item. Return raw totals only.\", :reads [:ingredient-list :usda-reference], :writes [:raw-data]