e it for speed, and prove the output is unchanged\n  - natural fit when the task is 'X is slow, make it faster WITHOUT changing what it produces' — a measure-then-change-then-re-measure loop, not a one-shot reshape\n  - outputs are typically a code edit to the hot path + a before/after measurement + evidence the observable output is byte-for-byte unchanged\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** measure to find the hot path BEFORE editing — optimize what the profile proves is slow, not what looks slow; premature edits to cold code add risk for no gain (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the slow region is not obvious AND a profiling/timing signal is available\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:input] :writes [:profile] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:profile :hot-path})}] [:llm {:reads [:profile :src] :writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src] :writes [:after-timing]}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :after-timing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** pin output-invariance with a check that compares optimized output to the original on representative inputs — a speedup that changes results is a regression, not an optimization (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the task explicitly requires 'without changing the output' (or correctness matters more than speed)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:optimized-src]}] [:code {:reads [:optimized-src :baseline-output] :writes [:invariance-check] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:invariance-check :outputs-equal})}] [:final {:keys [:optimized-src :invariance-check]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** optimizing by rewriting the log