    "## Suggested patterns from corpus\n\nThese are concrete EXAMPLES retrieved from the seed corpus based on classification of your task. Each example includes:\n  - WHY the candidate fits (reranker reasoning)\n  - The pattern's prose summary (seed `:summary`)\n  - Capabilities it provides\n  - Proven STRENGTHS — traits observed to work, each with a worked-example DSL snippet you can adapt\n  - Observed WEAKNESSES — failure modes others hit, with the recommended fix\n  - Representative uses where this pattern has shipped\n\nYou always have FOUR moves, and the references below are EVIDENCE for choosing — not a mandate:\n  - ADOPT — use a reference as-is when it is an EXACT fit.\n  - ADAPT — keep a reference's pattern, override the specifics, when it mostly fits.\n  - SPECIALIZE — mint a CHILD of the nearest reference (`mint-behavior!` with `:parent <that behavior-id>`) when the top hit is a BROAD shape rather than an exact fit; the child keeps the proven shape, pins your domain, and accrues evidence under the parent. This is the RECOMMENDED move when a match cleared threshold only on shape.\n  - MINT — mint a fresh behavior (`:parent nil`) when the task is genuinely novel and no reference is a true parent.\nA match clearing threshold does NOT mean adopt/adapt is your only option — weigh each reference's strengths AND its `:avoid-when` against THIS task, then pick the right move. Your job is the RIGHT tree for THIS task; the corpus is evidence, not gospel.\n\n### Structural patterns (top 3 from corpus retrieval)\n#### Top match — Briefing generation (confidence: 0.95)\nWhy this fits: The task requires synthesizing a cohesive narrative (a blameless postmortem) from multiple distinct sources (Varnish logs, chat exports, dashboard snapshots). This candidate's recommended pattern specifically handles chunking large documents, extracting key points/quotes, and using a final :llm stage for cross-chunk reasoning to identify themes and contradictions, which perfectly fits the requirement to 'reconcile the specific sequence of events' and deep-dive into root causes and contributing factors.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nBriefing generation is the canonical ResearchThenSynthesize instance: chunk → per-chunk :llm extraction → :aggregate → final synthesis :llm that produces a cohesive narrative. The synthesis stage's access to ALL per-chunk material enables cross-chunk reasoning (themes, contradictions). Always extract supporting quotes alongside summaries to support traceability. Watch context overflow — reduce per-chunk granularity if chunk count is high. Wire a grounding judge on the output when the briefing goes to a decision-maker. Use document-analysis (chunked-extraction) for structured triples; use chunked-extraction for terminal aggregation without synthesis.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - :sequence of :chunk-document → :map-each :llm (per-chunk extraction) → :aggregate (merged material) → :llm synthesizer (cohesive narrative)\n  - natural for any 'read X long material and produce a Y-paragraph briefing' work — executive briefings, research summaries, situation reports\n  - outputs are typically a single coherent text artifact organized by topic/section, not a structured key-value extraction\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** the synthesis stage's :llm has access to ALL per-chunk extracted material in :reads — produces a narrative that reasons across chunks rather than concatenating per-chunk summaries (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the briefing needs to identify cross-chunk patterns (recurring themes, contradictions, progression of an argument)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:chunk-document {:from :source :size 4000 :into :chunks}] [:map-each {:from :chunks :as :chunk :into :extracts :max-concurrency 3} [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:key-points :quotes]}]] [:aggregate {:from :extracts :writes [:all-points :all-quotes]}] [:llm {:reads [:all-points :all-quotes :briefing-prompt] :writes [:briefing]}] [:final {:keys [:briefing]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** extract specific QUOTES alongside summaries in the per-chunk step — the synthesis stage can then ground claims to source quotes, supporting traceability (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the briefing audience may verify claims against source material AND traceability matters\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:chunk] :writes [:key-points :supporting-quotes] :output-schemas {:key-points [:vector :string] :supporting-quotes [:vector :string]}}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** the synthesis stage's context can overflow when per-chunk extracts are too rich AND the chunk count is high — synthesizer truncates or hallucinates connections (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: per-chunk extraction emits >500 tokens per chunk AND chunk count is >15\n    - Recommended fix: either reduce per-chunk extraction granularity (key-points-only, no quotes) OR add an intermediate :code stage that selects top-K per-chunk extracts before synthesis\n\n  - **Failure mode:** without a grounding judge, the synthesis stage can fabricate connections between per-chunk extracts that aren't actually in the source — a 'plausible narrative' that doesn't survive verification (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the briefing is going to a decision-maker AND no grounding check is wired downstream\n    - Recommended fix: wire a grounding judge on the briefing output that checks each claim against the source quotes — catches fabricated connections before the briefing ships\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - executive briefing from a long research report: chunked extraction → final cohesive 3-paragraph briefing\n  - situation-report generation from a stream of incident logs: per-incident extraction → final narrative summary\n  - literature review summary: chunked paper extraction → final synthesized review section\n\n#### Alternative #1 — ResearchThenSynthesize (confidence: 0.92)\nWhy this fits: This is the parent tree-class for briefing generation. It correctly identifies the structural need for :chunk-document followed by :map-each extraction and a final :llm synthesis to integrate aggregated material into a cohesive narrative answer. It advances the intent by providing the necessary structure for cross-document reasoning required by the incident report.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nResearchThenSynthesize is a 4-stage shape: :chunk-document → :map-each [:llm extract] → :aggregate → final :llm synthesis. The final :llm integrates the aggregated per-chunk material into a cohesive narrative answer. Choose it when the answer requires reasoning across the whole document AND the output is a synthesis (briefing, summary, recommendation), not a flat extraction. Distinct from MapReduce (which uses :code for deterministic mechanical reduction) and from ChunkedExtraction (whose terminal step is the :aggregate merge). Watch the synthesis token budget — aggressive per-chunk extraction can overflow the final context — and pair with a grounding judge if claims must be traceable.\n\n\n#### Alternative #2 (confidence: 0.75)\nWhy this fits: The task involves a sequence of steps (extract timeline, analyze root cause, quantify impact, list action items), which fits an ETL pipeline's stage-gate structure. The strength of using :code for deterministic formatting at the tail fits the requirement to produce a specific technical internal report with listed action items.\n\nPattern guidance (seed `:summary`):\nETL pipelines chain 2-4 sequential stages where each stage's :writes feed the next stage's :reads. The proven shape uses :llm for content transformation and :code for deterministic formatting/aggregation at the tail. Always declare :output-schemas on intermediates so shape mismatches identify the offending stage, not the downstream consumer. Collapse very short successive stages into a single :llm to save tokens. Avoid when work is per-chunk independent (use chunked-extraction) or stages can run in parallel (use :parallel).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - ordered chain of typically 2-4 stages where each stage transforms the previous stage's output into a richer form\n  - natural fit for raw-input → normalized → enriched → emit-ready flows\n  - each stage's :reads consume the previous stage's :writes — explicit data dependency tracking\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** explicit per-stage :reads/:writes dependency chain makes ETL debuggable — failures isolate to the stage that produced the broken intermediate (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: input requires multiple distinct transformation passes AND each pass produces a named intermediate (normalized, enriched, formatted)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:raw-input] :writes [:normalized]}] [:llm {:reads [:normalized :reference-data] :writes [:enriched]}] [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:emit-ready] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:emit-ready (clojure.string/join \",\" enriched)})}] [:final {:keys [:emit-ready]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** use :code (NOT :llm) for the final emit-format stage when the format is deterministic — saves tokens and avoids drift in field names (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final stage is mechanical formatting (CSV row assembly, JSON envelope wrapping, fixed-width record padding)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:code {:reads [:enriched] :writes [:row] :fn (fn [{:keys [enriched]}] {:row (clojure.string/join \",\" (map :v enriched))})}]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** ETL chains break when an intermediate stage silently emits a different shape than the next stage expects — typed :output-schemas catch the shape mismatch at the wrong stage (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: intermediate stages don't declare :output-schemas and downstream stages assume specific keys\n    - Recommended fix: always declare :output-schemas on each intermediate stage AND validate at the next stage's :reads — push the shape contract upstream so the failing stage is identified, not the consumer\n\n  - **Failure mode:** 5+ stage chains burn tokens that a single multi-instruction :llm could cover when stages are short (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: individual stages are 1-2 sentence transformations AND there's no need to inspect intermediates\n    - Recommended fix: collapse 3+ short successive :llm stages into a single :llm with a multi-step instruction and structured :output-schemas for each intermediate\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - customer record normalization: parse → dedupe-fields → enrich-from-CRM → emit-warehouse-row\n  - log line parsing: tokenize → classify-event-type → annotate-with-context → emit-structured-event\n  - translation pipeline: clean-input → translate → polish-style → emit-final-text\n\n### Behavioral competencies (top 4 from corpus retrieval)\n1. Behavioral: Synthesis (confidence: 0.85)\n   Why this fits: The task requires integrating multiple inputs (logs, chats, dashboards, triage notes) into a 'cohesive artifact' (the postmortem report). Synthesis's recommended pattern of using a ':code-built outline' to ensure coverage is perfect for the requested detailed timeline and sections (root cause, impact, action items).\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Synthesis integrates multiple structured inputs into a single cohesive artifact (narrative / report / recommendation). Always provide the synthesizer with inputs structured by source-id (not free-form concat) so attribution stays intact. For long artifacts, scaffold via a :code-built outline that the :llm fills section-by-section — preserves coverage. NEVER synthesize >5 substantive inputs in one call: do hierarchical clustering instead. Declare :output-schemas on the synthesis output when downstream stages (critique / validation) need to verify specific sections. Composes into briefing-generation, comparative-summary, and sequential-pipeline shells. Avoid when the task is ideation (new options) or critique (checking against criteria).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given multiple structured inputs (findings, extracts, source quotes), produce a single integrated artifact — narrative, report, recommendation\n  - natural fit at the tail of a tree where multiple parallel branches converge\n  - outputs are typically a single string (or small structured map) where the structure of the inputs has been collapsed into coherent prose\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** make the synthesizer's :reads contain the per-source extractions structured by source-id rather than a free-form concat — supports apples-to-apples reference and avoids the model losing track of which finding came from where (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: synthesis needs to attribute claims to sources OR balance per-source weight\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:llm {:reads [:by-source] :writes [:narrative] :output-schemas {:narrative :string}}]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for long final artifacts, scaffold the synthesis with a :code node that pre-builds a structured outline from the inputs, then have the :llm fill each section — preserves coverage of all inputs (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the final artifact is multi-section AND each section maps to a distinct cluster of inputs\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:code {:reads [:findings] :writes [:outline] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:outline (mapv :type (:findings inputs))})}] [:llm {:reads [:findings :outline] :writes [:narrative]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** synthesizing >5 distinct inputs in a single :llm call exceeds context — the model drops inputs from the middle of the context window silently (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: input count >5 AND each input is substantive\n    - Recommended fix: hierarchical synthesis: cluster inputs into 2-3 groups → per-group :llm synthesis → final :llm integrates the group narratives\n\n  - **Failure mode:** free-form synthesis output (no :output-schemas) makes downstream stages (critique / validation) work on unstructured prose — they can't easily check coverage or shape (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: downstream stages need to verify specific sections are present\n    - Recommended fix: declare a structured :output-schemas on the synthesis (e.g., :map with :summary :recommendations :open-questions) — downstream critique/validation can verify each named field\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - multi-source briefing: extracts from N articles → single executive summary with citations\n  - comparative report: per-vendor extractions → structured comparison + recommendation\n  - investigation conclusion: per-witness extractions → integrated timeline + assessment\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent 2bce84a1-d186-3892-8131-c19b59e4543e)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n2. Behavioral: Investigation explains WHY a system (confidence: 0.95)\n   Why this fits: This task is a textbook case of Investigation: explaining WHY a system (global cache) is misbehaving. The candidate's recommended pattern of 'enumerate candidate hypotheses upfront' and 'collect evidence to rule out alternatives' directly maps to the user's need to reconcile logs, chat exports, and dashboards to find a root cause (VCL regex) and contributing factors (lack of staging).\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Investigation explains WHY a system is misbehaving. Best implemented as an explicit hypothesis-then-evidence shape: enumerate candidate causes from the symptom UPFRONT, then collect evidence to rule out alternatives, then converge on the root cause + recommended fix. ALWAYS pass ambient context (logs / code / tests / stack traces) in :reads — context-free investigation produces vacuous output. For large evidence sets (multi-file logs, large codebases), compose into chunked-extraction shells so per-chunk evidence collection parallelizes. Composes into sequential-pipeline (canonical hypothesis chain), fallback-recovery (try-one-path-then-another when initial hypothesis fails), and chunked-extraction (investigating large evidence sets). Distinct from Research (which gathers external material to seed new work, not explain existing breakage) and Analysis (which reasons over already-extracted items, not raw symptoms). Avoid when the task is exploration without a specific failure to explain.\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given symptoms + ambient context (logs, code, test artifacts, stack traces), produce a structured hypothesis with supporting evidence and a recommended fix or next step\n  - natural fit when the goal is to explain WHY something is broken — distinct from Research (gather new material) and Analysis (reason over already-extracted items)\n  - outputs are typically a single root-cause statement + supporting evidence references + a recommended-fix or next-step\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** enumerate candidate hypotheses UPFRONT from the symptom before collecting evidence — separates 'what could be wrong' from 'what is wrong' so evidence rules out rather than confirms the first guess (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the failure mode space is bounded AND the model can enumerate likely causes from the symptom alone\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:symptom :context] :writes [:candidate-hypotheses]}] [:llm {:reads [:candidate-hypotheses :context] :writes [:ruled-out :remaining]}] [:llm {:reads [:remaining :context] :writes [:root-cause :recommended-fix]}] [:final {:keys [:root-cause :recommended-fix]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** for investigations over large evidence sets (multi-file logs, large codebases), chunk the evidence and run per-chunk hypothesis-evidence collection via :map-each so per-chunk reasoning parallelizes and the aggregator sees per-chunk findings tagged with their source (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: evidence is too large to fit in a single :llm context AND per-chunk findings can be aggregated without losing per-chunk attribution\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:map-each {:items :evidence-chunks :as :chunk :max-concurrency 3 :writes [:per-chunk-findings]} [:llm {:reads [:chunk :symptom] :writes [:per-chunk-findings] :output-schemas {:per-chunk-findings [:vector [:map [:hypothesis :string] [:supporting-evidence :string] [:chunk-id :string]]]}}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** single-pass investigation on a complex symptom converges on the first plausible hypothesis without ruling out alternatives — produces overconfident root-cause claims that fail when the true cause was the second hypothesis (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symptom has multiple plausible causes AND the cost of acting on the wrong root cause is high\n    - Recommended fix: explicit multi-hypothesis enumeration upfront; an intermediate :llm stage must rule out at least ONE candidate hypothesis with evidence before convergence to a root cause is allowed\n\n  - **Failure mode:** investigation without ambient context (just the symptom + nothing else) produces vacuous 'could be X / could be Y' output the consumer can't act on — the model has no evidence to rule anything in or out (confidence 1.00, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: context (logs / code / tests / stack traces) is available in the system but not passed in :reads\n    - Recommended fix: always pass ambient context explicitly in :reads; if the context is too large for a single :llm node, compose into chunked-extraction via :map-each so per-chunk evidence reaches the hypothesis-evidence stage\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - investigate why an integration test is flaky: enumerate timing / shared-state / environment hypotheses; rule out via test logs + run history; recommend the deterministic fix\n  - generate property-based tests that exercise edge cases — property test generation IS investigation, finding inputs that break invariants\n  - debug a memory leak in a long-running service: enumerate allocator / reference-cycle / cache-bloat hypotheses; rule out via heap snapshots + allocation profiles; recommend the targeted fix\n  - review a post-incident report for missing root-cause analysis: enumerate plausible causes the report didn't address; surface the gap with evidence from the timeline\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete DSL snippet>\"\n                                     :confidence 0.7\n                                     :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :weaknesses [{:trait \"<what fails AND why>\"\n                                      :avoid-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                      :recommended-alternative \"<concrete fix>\"\n                                      :confidence 0.7\n                                      :evidence-count 1}]\n                        :representative-uses [\"<concrete task this child shipped on>\"]\n                        :avoid-when [\"<concrete anti-context>\"]\n                        :summary \"<2-3 sentences naming the domain + load-bearing trait + when to prefer this over the parent>\"\n                        :version 1\n                        :consolidated-from-event-count 0}\n                       :parent 760be698-0bb8-3a5a-a2bd-1d45445a5861)\n\n   - MINT-ADJACENT — if your task is related-but-DISTINCT (not a child of this behavior), mint an adjacent behavior the same way with `:parent nil` (or under whichever listed reference is the true nearest parent).\n   :strengths/:weaknesses are VECTORS OF MAPS (principle-shaped: :trait + context-guard + concrete recommended action + :confidence + :evidence-count) — a vector of bare strings fails schema validation; the rejection is returned in iteration history for correction.\n\n3. Behavioral: Bug-diagnosis explains WHY code (confidence: 0.90)\n   Why this fits: As a child of Investigation, this is highly relevant because the incident involves a code-level failure (VCL regex). The strength of 'ruling out candidate causes with evidence' is crucial for a 'blameless' postmortem that needs to be 'technical and focused on the system failure' rather than assumptions.\n\n   Guidance (seed `:summary`):\n   Bug-diagnosis explains WHY code is broken and fixes it — a reproduce-then-bisect-then-fix loop sharpening the Investigation parent for the code-bug case. The load-bearing traits are establishing a DETERMINISTIC reproduction before attempting a fix (a flaky symptom must be pinned to a reliable trigger or you can't know the fix worked) and enumerating candidate causes to rule out with evidence before converging (don't fix the first plausible guess). Prefer this child of Investigation whenever the task is 'this test/behavior is broken, find why and fix it'. Avoid when building new functionality from a spec (code-building, no failure to diagnose) or doing exploratory research with no specific failure (Research).\n\nCapabilities:\n  - given a failing/flaky test or a wrong-behavior bug report + the relevant code, reproduce it deterministically, isolate the root cause, and produce the minimal fix\n  - natural fit when the goal is 'this code is broken, find WHY and fix it' — a reproduce-then-bisect-then-fix loop, distinct from greenfield code-building\n  - outputs are typically a deterministic repro, a root-cause statement tied to a specific code location, and the minimal change that makes the repro pass\n\nStrengths (proven traits — these patterns have been observed to work; mimic where they fit, adapt as needed):\n  - **Trait:** establish a DETERMINISTIC reproduction before attempting a fix — a flaky/intermittent symptom must be pinned to a reliable trigger first, or you cannot know the fix worked (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the bug is intermittent or its trigger is not yet understood (e.g. an intermittent nil from shared state / timing)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:reads [:symptom :code] :writes [:candidate-causes]}] [:code {:reads [:candidate-causes] :writes [:repro] :fn (fn [{:keys [inputs]}] {:repro :deterministic-failing-case})}] [:llm {:reads [:repro :code] :writes [:root-cause :fix]}] [:final {:keys [:root-cause :fix]}]]\n      ```\n\n  - **Trait:** enumerate candidate causes upfront and rule them out with evidence before converging — bisect the search space rather than fixing the first plausible guess (which often masks the real cause) (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Good when: the symptom has multiple plausible causes (timing, shared state, environment, off-by-one)\n    - Worked example DSL (corpus reference — adapt to your task):\n      ```clojure\n      [:sequence [:llm {:writes [:hypotheses]}] [:llm {:reads [:hypotheses :evidence] :writes [:ruled-out :remaining]}] [:llm {:reads [:remaining] :writes [:root-cause :minimal-fix]}]]\n      ```\n\nWeaknesses (observed failure modes — avoid these patterns, apply the recommended fix where applicable):\n  - **Failure mode:** jumping straight to a code edit on the first hypothesis (a code-building instinct) without a deterministic repro produces a fix that may paper over the symptom while the real defect remains — and you cannot prove it is fixed (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the symptom is intermittent OR the root cause is not yet evidenced\n    - Recommended fix: reproduce deterministically first, name the root cause with evidence, then make the minimal change and re-run the repro\n\n  - **Failure mode:** an over-broad 'fix' that rewrites surrounding code while chasing a bug introduces new regressions and obscures which change actually resolved the defect (confidence 0.70, evidence-count 1)\n    - Avoid when: the bug is localized and a targeted change suffices\n    - Recommended fix: scope the fix to the proven root cause; keep unrelated code untouched so the repro pinpoints the effective change\n\nRepresentative uses (concrete tasks this pattern has shipped on):\n  - debug why the integration test checkout-flow-test fails intermittently with a nil cart-id and make it deterministic\n  - fix the off-by-one bug in the pagination loop so the final page renders its last row instead of dropping it\n\n   Adopt / adapt / SPECIALIZE / mint-adjacent — use the evidence above, not the score alone:\n   - ADOPT this pattern as-is if it is an EXACT fit for your task.\n   - ADAPT it (keep the shape, override the specifics) if it mostly fits.\n   - SPECIALIZE — if this is a BROAD fit rather than an exact one (its `:summary`/strengths describe a more general shape than your task, or its `:avoid-when` flags your context), mint a domain-specialized CHILD of THIS behavior. The child keeps the parent's proven shape but pins your domain, gets a STABLE derived id, and ACCRUES evidence under the parent (it is retrievable on subsequent classify-behaviors calls):\n\n       (mint-behavior! \"<short-kebab-name>\"\n                       {:capabilities [\"<concrete action verb + object>\"]\n                        :strengths [{:trait \"<what works AND why>\"\n                                     :good-when \"<context guard>\"\n                                     :recommended-pattern \"<concrete